= zeszyt_urbaniaka
# tlo: karczma_zeszyt
In July, just before the harvest, Urbaniak called the people together outside the inn. He came in his britzka, wearing a linen coat, and beside him sat the foreman and Pawlik the gamekeeper with a double-barrelled gun across his knees.{ oblawa_wilk == rzucil_kamieniem: Pawlik picked out Jaromir in the crowd at once and touched a finger to his own cheek, where Jaromir bore his scar, as if to let him know that he remembered too.}
“The road to the woods must be ready before winter,” said Urbaniak, without getting down. “Without it the sawmill will stand idle, and if the sawmill stands idle, there’ll be neither wages nor flour for the hungry gap. So every cottage will give one man for a week to work on the road. Whoever owes me will work off his debt.” He took from his pocket his debt book in its oilcloth cover and laid it on his knee. “Whoever doesn’t, I’ll pay in silver. And the wayside shrine by the cutting will be moved aside. It pushes too far into the road.” # pokaz: zeszyt
A murmur ran through the crowd. The shrine by the cutting was {kapliczki_drogi == obronil_kapliczke:the very one Jaromir had defended years before, when Urbaniak’s men were digging at the edge of the forest—the last of those that stood by the woods.|the only one still standing by the woods—old Staś had made his grandsons put it up anew from the remains of the dug-up posts when they began to clear the cutting.}
Old Staś stepped out in front of the people, leaning on his stick. Behind him stood the smith, and beside him Antek, with Franek in his arms, for Jadwisia was out in the field. Szymon, Józek’s father, who since spring had kept the night watch at the sawmill, hung his head and edged aside.
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
*   [Stand up to Urbaniak in front of everyone.]
    ~ szarwark = sprzeciw_przy_karczmie
    “You’ll not touch the shrines,” said Jaromir loudly. “Neither this one nor any other. And none of us will hack a road into the forest.”
    Several of the men muttered their agreement. Urbaniak studied him for a moment, then opened the debt book, licked his finger and turned over a few pages.
    “Your father,” he read out, “borrowed from me for grain when he was laid up with his leg. And a second time to see him through the hungry gap. Good of you to remind me.”
    On Monday the foreman led Jaromir’s cow out of the byre. His mother stood in the doorway weeping without a sound, and his father gripped the door frame so hard that his knuckles went white. But when the first day of work came, not a single man from Zapadlina went to the cutting. # pokaz: krowa # dalej
*   [Sign on for the road work—the family must be fed.]
    ~ szarwark = robota_na_przesiece
    Jaromir walked up to the britzka and gave the foreman his hand. Someone in the crowd hissed “Judas”, and old Staś spat at Jaromir’s feet.
    For a week he hewed at the cutting alongside the men from Bukowiec. The trunks were hard as stone and rang under the axe, and every morning new shoots sprang from each stump, green and sticky. On the last day, towards evening, a slender spruce that no one had yet cut into toppled of its own accord—straight onto a farmhand working next to Jaromir. The lad lived, but his leg never knitted properly again. # pokaz: swierk
    Jaromir brought home a handful of silver. Hanka took it without a word and put it away in the chest. The resin that had coated his hands all that week would not come off with water or with ash; a dark stain on the palm of his right hand stayed there a long time. # dalej
*   [Go out with Antek by night and pull up the stakes.]
    ~ szarwark = paliki_w_bagnie
    They went after midnight, the two of them, with spades and a sack. The stakes were already putting down roots—they had to be prised out of the earth like young saplings, and each one came free with a soft, sucking sound that made the skin crawl. By dawn they had pulled up every last one, from the sawmill to the marshes, and thrown them all into the bog. # pokaz: noc
    In the morning the gamekeeper was waiting by the watchman’s hut. Szymon, Józek’s father, kept his eyes on the ground and said nothing, but Pawlik knew whose work it was all the same—Antek’s trousers were caked with mud to the knees. They took him off to the lock-up in Bukowiec for a fortnight. No one pointed a finger at Jaromir. To have Antek let out before the harvest, Jadwisia carried her wedding beads to Bukowiec to pay the fine—the only ones she had from her mother. # dalej
