# tlo: noc_ognia
The harvest was over by the middle of August. The stubble cracked with drought, and at night lightning flickered over the forest without any thunder.
{ uboze == nakarmione:
    One night Jaromir was woken by a tug at his hair—once, twice, hard, until tears sprang to his eyes. By the bed stood a tiny old man in a little red cap, his beard wound round his hen’s feet, trembling all over.
    “Fire,” he squeaked. “Fire coming from the woods. Get up, master.”
    ~ zapiski += uboze_3
    When Jaromir burst out in front of the cottage, it was still dark over the village. Only after a moment, right at the edge of the forest, did the first light flare up.
- else:
    One night Jaromir was woken by the bell from Bukowiec—uneven, ragged, as when someone strikes it blindly. When he burst out in front of the cottage, the sky towards the forest was already red.
}
The shrines were burning. First the one by the cutting, then the one on the Bukowiec road, beyond the stream, and {noc_na_kladce == latarnia_na_wodzie:the birch cross by the footbridge|{noc_na_kladce == figurka_dla_stwora:the one by the fallen footbridge|the one by the footbridge}}—one after another, like candles in church lit from a single wick. # pokaz: ogien
A moment later flames shot up by the stream—the sawmill. They leapt above the roof of the shed, higher than the tallest spruces, and by their light Jaromir saw that the door of the watchman’s hut was shut, and that something was moving at its little window. # pokaz: tartak
Szymon. Józek’s father.
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
*   [Run to the shrine by the cutting—it can still be saved.]
    ~ noc_ognia = ratowal_kapliczke
    He ran across the stubble, panting like a bellows. The shrine’s little roof was already catching, but the post still stood. Jaromir tore off his shirt, soaked it in the trough by the well and beat at the flames with it, and when that was no use he flung sand on them with his bare hands. He carried the saint out of the fire—scorched, its head cracked—and laid it on the grass. The post held. # pokaz: kapliczka
    The sawmill burned to the bare earth. Szymon was dragged out by men from Bukowiec when the roof was already falling in. He lived, but the fire had eaten half his face and both his hands. That winter Józek stood cap in hand outside the church in Bukowiec, for his father could no longer hold so much as a spoon, and there were five mouths to feed at home. # dalej
*   [Run to the sawmill—there is a man in the watchman’s hut.]
    ~ noc_ognia = ratowal_szymona
    The door of the watchman’s hut had been barred from outside with a pole. Jaromir knocked it away with his axe, plunged into the smoke and dragged Szymon out by the collar, half-senseless, his eyebrows singed. A moment later the roof of the shed came down with a roar and a column of sparks went up into the sky. # pokaz: szymon
    When Jaromir turned round, only three red points still burned above the village. By morning nothing was left of the shrines but ashes.
    While they were bringing him round, Szymon kept saying the one thing over and over: that someone had barred his door. He would not say who. # dalej
*   { uboze == nakarmione } [Rouse the village and send folk to the shrine, and run to the sawmill alone.]
    ~ noc_ognia = zbudzil_wies
    Jaromir pounded on every window along the way, shouting until he was hoarse. Before the fire could devour the shrine by the cutting, the smith and Antek were already there with buckets. He himself ran to the sawmill, knocked away the pole someone had wedged against the door of the watchman’s hut and dragged Szymon out of the smoke. # pokaz: wiadra
    The shrine by the cutting survived. Szymon lived. But as Jaromir walked home he could not bend his fingers—both palms had been burned to the raw flesh by the red-hot door latch. Hanka dressed them every day until All Saints’, and all autumn long he could hold neither flail nor axe. # dalej
