    ~ droga_do_boru = true
    “We’ll take it,” said Jaromir. “But you’ll burn the debt book today, sir, in front of us. We’ll do the work.”
    Urbaniak hesitated, then tore the pages from the debt book and lit them from the smouldering ruins. People watched the black flakes drift up over the ashes of the sawmill, and no one rejoiced. # pokaz: zeszyt_plonie
    All autumn Zapadlina hacked the road deep into the forest. The trees came down heavily and unwillingly, and at night the stumps in the cutting creaked though there was no wind. Towards the end of November the road reached the first great pines—and there it stopped, for no one would cut any further, not even for gold. # dalej
*   [Refuse—the forest must not be touched.]
    “No,” said Jaromir. “Not a step further. Send your bailiff, sir.”
    The men were silent a long while. At last old Staś took off his cap, crossed himself and stood beside him. After him the smith, after the smith Antek, and after them the rest.
    The bailiff came in October. He led the cows out of six byres, and from the other cottages he carted off the grain. That winter Zapadlina was once again grinding birch bark for its bread. But the young wood by the forest, which had come on the night of the fire, did not grow another span—it stayed where it stood, at the line of the burnt shrines. # pokaz: komornik # dalej
